  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\"><em>Pro-vax</em>, published in 2025 by Peter Lang Verlag as part of the Medical Humanities series, addresses vaccine activism and public engagement around vaccination. The work has received one scholarly citation as of 2026, appearing in a qualitative study on COVID-19 vaccine acceptance across Southeast Asia and the United Kingdom. The book aligns with UN Sustainable Development Goal 3 (Good health and well-being) and holds measurable adoption in academic libraries.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Samantha Vanderslott's <em>Pro-vax</em> opens a new research direction by centering positive activism and advocacy around vaccination\u2014a counterpoint to dominant narratives that focus on vaccine hesitancy and resistance. The book situates itself within the Medical Humanities: Criticism and Creativity series at Peter Lang, joining recent volumes on embodied knowledge and health-related topics. This positioning reflects a scholarly commitment to interdisciplinary treatment of contemporary public health challenges.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The work's initial scholarly uptake is evident in a 2026 citation in <em>PLOS Global Public Health</em>, which drew on Vanderslott's framework for a qualitative study examining vaccine acceptance in Indonesia, Nepal, Vietnam, and the United Kingdom. That citing study involved researchers from the University of Indonesia, University of Oxford, and Kathmandu University, with emphasis on thematic analysis and psychological factors shaping vaccine uptake. The geographic and institutional scope of this early citation suggests the book addresses questions relevant to global vaccine policy and implementation across diverse contexts.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library holdings data indicates modest but real adoption in academic collections. The book is catalogued in WorldCat as a public health resource, with at least four institutional holdings recorded, indicating acquisition by university and research libraries. This foundational adoption signal\u2014though not yet manifested in widespread teaching references\u2014positions the work as a reference point for scholars and practitioners engaged with vaccination communication and medical humanities.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">At this early stage, the record shows a work meeting its intended scholarly audience rather than dominant public discourse. The absence of event-based attention data does not indicate lack of impact; rather, it reflects the work's positioning within academic and professional networks in public health and medical humanities. As scholarship on vaccination activism continues to develop, this book's conceptual contribution to reframing pro-vaccine engagement may gain broader traction within and across disciplinary boundaries.</p>",
